WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.160
So in our scene, I talked about the directional light,

2
00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.320
and then what you can do to maybe edit it on the fly

3
00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.960
and some of the properties and settings that you can use

4
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:12.259
and that are at your disposal.

5
00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:14.820
What we're gonna do now is look at a few other lights.

6
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.019
The next one we'll look at is the spotlight.

7
00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:19.300
Now the spotlight is great and works very much

8
00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:22.379
like your ones that you may be familiar with

9
00:00:22.379 --> 00:00:23.859
in your DCCs.

10
00:00:23.859 --> 00:00:26.699
It has our intensity control,

11
00:00:26.699 --> 00:00:29.100
but we also have our intensity units.

12
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.500
We can choose what we want.

13
00:00:30.539 --> 00:00:31.800
The default is candlest,

14
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:34.140
but you can choose whatever is convenient for you.

15
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:36.500
You'll see that we can control the color

16
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.740
as well as the attenuation radius.

17
00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:40.539
Now the attenuation radius is great

18
00:00:40.539 --> 00:00:42.340
because it allows us to control

19
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.460
how far the influence for this light is going to be

20
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.380
for our environment and the objects nearby.

21
00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:51.659
So the inner cone angle allows us also to control that,

22
00:00:51.659 --> 00:00:54.380
and you'll see it expand and contract

23
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.939
as I move it around, allowing us to control this

24
00:00:56.939 --> 00:00:59.539
if we want it to look like a particular type of spotlight

25
00:00:59.539 --> 00:01:02.140
with a different type of drop-off and fall-off

26
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.540
from the inside to the out, that's up to you.

27
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.280
But you'll also notice we also have our source radius.

28
00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:10.439
Now the source radius, again,

29
00:01:10.439 --> 00:01:13.780
can control how that reflective object is being handled

30
00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:15.640
and being affected by that spotlight.

31
00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.920
Let's go and duplicate the spotlight for a second.

32
00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:19.680
We'll do this a little bit more with a point light

33
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.099
so it makes it a little more obvious,

34
00:01:21.099 --> 00:01:23.359
but you can kind of get an idea how this works.

35
00:01:23.359 --> 00:01:24.599
I'm gonna keep my finger on the Alt key

36
00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:26.019
and move this around,

37
00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.579
and let's see if we can put this on a reflective object

38
00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:30.819
so you can kind of get a better idea how this works.

39
00:01:30.819 --> 00:01:32.939
So if I move this a bit closer,

40
00:01:32.939 --> 00:01:38.780
we can see the hotspots coming off the spotlight,

41
00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:39.980
and we'll move it up so you can see it

42
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:41.019
a little bit better here

43
00:01:41.019 --> 00:01:43.019
at the very top of our lighting,

44
00:01:43.019 --> 00:01:44.579
and you'll see the hotspot right here

45
00:01:44.579 --> 00:01:47.000
where my cursor's pulsing.

46
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:48.480
Now you'll notice, again,

47
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:50.400
we have the inner cone angle and the outer cone angle

48
00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.040
where we can adjust the size of this light

49
00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.920
and how the fall-off is controlled

50
00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.980
from the outside and the inside.

51
00:01:56.980 --> 00:01:58.819
But you'll also notice, then,

52
00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:01.900
our source radius here that we can control.

53
00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:03.579
So I can control my source radius,

54
00:02:03.579 --> 00:02:06.700
and you're gonna see that reflective surface object

55
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.780
get a bit bigger.

56
00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.740
It's almost like we're adjusting the size of this light.

57
00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:12.659
Kind of not, but at the same time,

58
00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:15.860
it's almost like you can think of it as overexposure,

59
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.680
making this have a stronger hotspot in a way,

60
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.900
but locally to the light, it's actually kind of cool.

61
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:24.620
But you'll also notice we have a soft source radius,

62
00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:25.740
and you can see this a little bit better

63
00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:27.939
with the spotlight, but you can see as I do this,

64
00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:29.240
it is softening up.

65
00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.500
So that reflective hotspot that's happening isn't so sharp.

66
00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.259
And again, that's kind of the same effect you had here.

67
00:02:36.259 --> 00:02:38.300
We didn't see it quite as well

68
00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.780
with our directional light,

69
00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:42.639
but it's definitely very visible

70
00:02:42.639 --> 00:02:43.860
when it comes to the spotlight

71
00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:49.250
and also the point light.

72
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.090
Awesome.

73
00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.430
So we kind of have some of the same settings

74
00:02:52.430 --> 00:02:53.490
we had before.

75
00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:54.770
We'll have the use temperature,

76
00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:57.330
effects of the world, cast shadows,

77
00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:58.650
indirect lighting intensity,

78
00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.270
and also volumetric scatter intensity

79
00:03:01.270 --> 00:03:02.509
when it comes to the fog.

80
00:03:02.509 --> 00:03:05.370
But this spotlight is great and works very much

81
00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:08.330
like your other DCC spotlights.

82
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:09.569
It's pretty straightforward,

83
00:03:09.569 --> 00:03:14.009
but we also have an IES profile that we can load in here.

84
00:03:14.009 --> 00:03:14.849
And those are some of the things

85
00:03:14.849 --> 00:03:21.460
we'll talk about in other classes.

86
00:03:21.460 --> 00:03:22.460
Let's go ahead and take a moment

87
00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:26.840
and take a look at our skylight.

88
00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:28.740
So the skylight in our scene,

89
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.759
and I'm going to select it for a moment.

90
00:03:30.759 --> 00:03:32.240
You'll see, and let me pull him up

91
00:03:32.319 --> 00:03:33.159
and just pull him over

92
00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:35.639
so you're a little bit more familiar with the icon.

93
00:03:35.639 --> 00:03:36.919
It looks like,

94
00:03:36.919 --> 00:03:39.479
let's move away from the other objects here.

95
00:03:39.479 --> 00:03:41.000
He looks like kind of a dome

96
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.090
with two, like three little hash kind of tick marks here

97
00:03:46.289 --> 00:03:47.770
coming down from it.

98
00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.090
And this is our skylight in Unreal.

99
00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:53.770
And the skylight allows us to quickly get some GI.

100
00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.569
Now, when you're working with Lumen by default,

101
00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:56.550
this should be on,

102
00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.729
but you want to turn on real time,

103
00:03:58.729 --> 00:04:00.610
effects real time here.

104
00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:02.530
And you're able to also load

105
00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:04.610
whatever cube map that you want.

106
00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.650
Now, what we're using is right now,

107
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.250
it is using our scene information

108
00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:12.889
and the GI bouncing around.

109
00:04:12.889 --> 00:04:14.530
And the only reason it's stuttering a little bit

110
00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:15.490
when I move my camera,

111
00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.370
I don't think it's your playback here.

112
00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.410
It's just, I'm a really bad driver.

113
00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.410
The only reason that this is set up here

114
00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.930
is by default, it's plucking from your scene

115
00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.170
and bouncing around

116
00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:28.490
and getting that information from the GI

117
00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:30.189
and applying it to this.

118
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.709
Like it's almost making its own internal HDRI map

119
00:04:34.709 --> 00:04:35.949
and applying it to your scene.

120
00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:37.529
You can think of it that way.

121
00:04:37.529 --> 00:04:39.189
But if you wanted to,

122
00:04:39.189 --> 00:04:40.829
you can make it so that you're turning off

123
00:04:40.829 --> 00:04:42.750
the real time calculation for that.

124
00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.110
And then you can go in here and say,

125
00:04:45.110 --> 00:04:49.589
SLS, our little specified cube map,

126
00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:50.949
we can put in here

127
00:04:50.949 --> 00:04:53.550
so that we're using our skylight cube map,

128
00:04:53.550 --> 00:04:54.470
specified cube map.

129
00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.149
We can choose whatever we want.

130
00:04:56.149 --> 00:04:57.310
And you'll see when I turn it off,

131
00:04:57.310 --> 00:04:59.930
it now isn't grabbing it from our information,

132
00:05:00.529 --> 00:05:02.329
from our sky or light bouncing around.

133
00:05:02.329 --> 00:05:04.470
So it's not quite as cool looking.

134
00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.370
It doesn't seem to,

135
00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.389
it kind of separates itself from the rest of the scene.

136
00:05:08.389 --> 00:05:09.250
But if I wanted to,

137
00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.730
I can now pluck one that we already have,

138
00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:15.610
or one that I can grab from such places as HDR Haven

139
00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:17.050
and load it into our scene.

140
00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:19.279
And it will load.

141
00:05:19.279 --> 00:05:21.819
Now, this is really up to you how you want to do this.

142
00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:23.879
I prefer using real time capture,

143
00:05:23.879 --> 00:05:25.319
but there will be times

144
00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:29.160
when I will need a specific GI coming in

145
00:05:29.199 --> 00:05:32.199
through this skylight

146
00:05:32.199 --> 00:05:33.819
and I'll want to pluck that in there.

147
00:05:33.819 --> 00:05:34.839
So every once in a while,

148
00:05:34.839 --> 00:05:36.980
I'll be like, hmm, this isn't working for me.

149
00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:39.040
I need to push these values.

150
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:40.800
And that's really helpful to have that.

151
00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:44.379
So you have source cube map angle too you can control.

152
00:05:44.379 --> 00:05:46.339
We don't have a cube map loaded in here,

153
00:05:46.339 --> 00:05:47.199
but if we did,

154
00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:49.680
we're able to go in here and control that.

155
00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:52.100
And I can do this briefly so you can get an idea.

156
00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:54.920
We can say, hey, turn off the real time there.

157
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:56.399
And then you can push this through

158
00:05:56.399 --> 00:05:57.639
and you can see the values change a little bit

159
00:05:57.639 --> 00:05:58.959
on our materials here.

160
00:05:58.959 --> 00:06:00.439
And a bit of the light.

161
00:06:00.439 --> 00:06:02.160
Now, remember this isn't like a directional light.

162
00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:04.040
So when you mess with some of these values,

163
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.240
that's gonna more of make it brighter or darker.

164
00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:08.540
It's a little more linear.

165
00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:09.860
There's an absolute control

166
00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.319
where the light's coming from per se.

167
00:06:12.319 --> 00:06:13.639
That's more where you would pair

168
00:06:13.639 --> 00:06:15.279
your directional light with it,

169
00:06:15.279 --> 00:06:18.180
with this tool to get it to work the way that you want.

170
00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:20.600
Let's put this back to real time calculation.

171
00:06:20.600 --> 00:06:22.000
So we have cube map resolution.

172
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:23.399
You can include that too,

173
00:06:23.399 --> 00:06:25.319
as well as sky distance threshold,

174
00:06:26.279 --> 00:06:29.000
how far out that's going to influence your world.

175
00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:32.360
And you can simply do that by changing your views here.

176
00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:34.839
And then if you wanted to in Unreal units,

177
00:06:34.839 --> 00:06:37.800
middle mouse drag and calculate your distance

178
00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:39.120
of where your objects are

179
00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:41.959
versus where you want your skylight to calculate them.

180
00:06:41.959 --> 00:06:45.420
And that can be pretty helpful for you as you need.

181
00:06:45.420 --> 00:06:48.040
So you can say, does it affect the world?

182
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:50.540
Does it cast shadows, your indirect light intensity,

183
00:06:50.540 --> 00:06:54.029
as well as how this is gonna relate to your fog.

184
00:06:54.029 --> 00:06:55.110
Awesome.

185
00:06:55.110 --> 00:06:59.300
So let's go and move on to a few more lights.

186
00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:00.819
And in the next video,

187
00:07:00.819 --> 00:07:04.300
we're gonna be talking about our point light,

188
00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:05.579
our rec light.

189
00:07:05.579 --> 00:07:07.660
And we're gonna mention a bit briefly

190
00:07:07.660 --> 00:07:11.319
about the HDR backdrop and the sun and sky.

191
00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:12.139
Thanks again.
